              "@id": "urn:uuid:05837d4e-4246-58bb-a4fb-8d90ff07a0a7",
              "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedString",
              "uco-observable:stringValue": "Case 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 1 of 16\n\nIN THE UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT\nFOR THE DISTRICT OF MARYLAND\n\nUNITED STATES OF AMERICA *\n*\nv. * CRIMINAL NO.\n* 8:24-cr-00187-LKG\nFODE SITAFA MARA *\n*\nDefendant *\n*\n*******\n\nUNITED STATES\u2019S SENTENCING MEMORANDUM\n\nThe United States of America, by and through Kelly O. Hayes, United States Attorney for\n\nthe District of Maryland, Ranganath Manthripragada and Brooke Oki, Assistant United States\n\nAttorneys, and Adam Braskich, Trial Attorney, hereby submits the government\u2019s sentencing\n\nmemorandum requesting that this Court sentence defendant Fode Sitafa Mara to a term of life\n\nimprisonment. 1 This recommendation aligns with the United States Sentencing Guidelines and is\n\nsupported by the factors in 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a), in that it provides just punishment, adequate\n\ndeterrence, and protection of the public. 2\n\nI. PROCEDURAL HISTORY\n\nOn June 6, 2024, Defendant Mara was indicted by a grand jury on seven counts: Counts\n\n1, 2, 4, 5, and 6, Aggravated Sexual Abuse of a Minor, in violation of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2241(c); Count\n\n3, Coercion and Enticement of a Minor, in violation of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2422(b); and Count 7,\n\nObstruction of Justice, in violation of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1512(c)(2). PSR \u00b6 1.\n\n1\nShould the Court sentence the defendant to a lesser term of imprisonment, the government\nrequests that it be followed by a lifetime term of supervised release.\n2\nThe Government filed a Motion for a Preliminary Order of Forfeiture (ECF No. 152) on\nFebruary 12, 2026.\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 2 of 16\n\nIn October 2025, Mara was tried by a jury, convicted of Counts 1-4, 6, and 7, and\n\nacquitted of Count 5. PSR. at \u00b6 2.\n\nII. FACTUAL BACKGROUND\n\nAs the government\u2019s evidence and witness testimony established at trial, for over a year,\n\nFode Mara took advantage of two deeply impoverished teenage girls, repeatedly sexually\n\nabusing them inside Residence 640, his government-provided home in Ouagadougou, Burkina\n\nFaso.\n\nFor Minor Victim 1 (MV1) and Minor Victim 2 (MV2), Residence 640 had once been a\n\nhappy place, providing rare respite from the misery of their daily lives. The girls lived in abject\n\npoverty, their own home consisting of two small, crude structures for shelter, an outdoor\n\nwaterspout for a shower, and a hole in the ground for a toilet. The victims\u2019 mother and brothers\n\nslept in a three-walled concrete space, while the girls were slightly more fortunate, able to sleep\n\non the ground in a small, cluttered, but fully enclosed room. Food was scarce, and the victims\n\nwould often allow their younger siblings to eat while they stayed hungry.\n\nWhen American diplomat Nancy Flores\u2014who resided at Residence 640 prior to the\n\ndefendant\u2014first met the victims, she saw an opportunity to help individuals in need. PSR at \u00b6 9.\n\nShe provided the victims and their family with support and friendship and often allowed them\n\ninto Residence 640, where they found nourishment, comfort, and safety. She asked for nothing in\n\nreturn.\n\nWhen the defendant first laid eyes on the victims, he saw an opportunity. He made his\n\nintentions known quickly, groping their breasts just the second time he was with them. PSR at \u00b6\n\n10. Mara knew he could make the girls dependent on him by providing support as Ms. Flores\n\nhad, and he stooped as low as he could, using their mother\u2019s life-threatening illness as an\n\n2\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 3 of 16\n\nopportunity to demand sex by telling MV1 he could not help her without receiving something in\n\nreturn. Mara also knew that Residence 640 was the perfect setting for his crimes. When his wife\n\nwas away at work, he could summon the victims there and find opportunities to take advantage\n\nof them. PSR at \u00b6 12. And he did exactly that, repeatedly and violently. PSR at \u00b6\u00b6 26-29. Mara\n\nphysically forced himself on both of his teenage victims, at times pushing them down, pinning\n\nthem against the bed, overpowering their physical resistance, and causing them to bleed from the\n\nmost intimate parts of their bodies. He was cavalier about raping them, afterward giving them a\n\npaltry amount of cash or offering to buy a sheep for their family.\n\nMara was also controlling of his victims. As the Court heard from Mara himself, he\n\nprovided them with phones and social media accounts so he could communicate with them, and\n\nhe maintained their passwords, presumably so he could monitor them. PSR at \u00b6 16-22. Mara\n\ntestified that when the victims learned he had access to their devices and changed their\n\npasswords, he responded by taking the phones away. The government, of course, also maintains\n\nthat Mara took the phones away because they contained evidence that he was manipulating and\n\nsexually abusing the victims. Particularly with MV1, Mara sent messages demanding her\n\nsubservience. See, e.g., Gov\u2019t Trial Ex. 107 at 13 (\u201cYou are supposed to be here to satisfy me not\n\nto challenge me. I know you can do it but you refuse to.\u201d); at 15 (\u201cWhy do you keep apologizing,\n\nbut never change[?]\u201d); and at 19-20 (\u201c[S]end me a very seductive picture of you . . . I will have\n\nmy photos tonight. No questions.\u201d).\n\nMara\u2019s sexual assaults were repeated and spaced across many months. They were also\n\nrepresentative of Mara\u2019s sexual interest in children, which the government proved through\n\nMara\u2019s web history, which showed he searched for and viewed child pornography. See Gov\u2019t\n\nTrial Ex. 91-92; PSR at \u00b6 24.\n\n3\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 4 of 16\n\nWhen confronted with the evidence of his crimes, from the earliest stages of the\n\ninvestigation through trial, Mara responded consistently: by lying, deflecting blame, and\n\nattempting to obstruct justice. Confronted with the condom he used when sexually assaulting\n\nMV1, Mara fabricated a sexual affair with his housekeeper and asked the housekeeper to lie to\n\ninvestigators on his behalf. PSR at \u00b6 23. He told the jury that the housekeeper enticed him that\n\nmorning \u2013 and that, absurdly, she was \u201chalf naked, as usual\u201d when she awakened him for sex.\n\nFaced with his child pornography internet searches, Mara offered a convoluted explanation about\n\nhow he was trying to stop receiving unsolicited emails. Confronted with his manipulative and\n\nsexually explicit WhatsApp conversation with MV1, Mara claimed he was having a conversation\n\nnot with MV1 or MV2 (whose pictures appeared in the chat), but with the mother of a girl named\n\n\u201cAlice,\u201d whose name he could not remember. He also told the jury that the government doctored\n\nthe WhatsApp chat to make it appear he had sent a pornographic GIF when, he claimed, it was\n\nactually Alice\u2019s mother who sent it to him. (This was after Mara stipulated to the authenticity of\n\nthat exhibit \u2013 see Gov\u2019t Trial Ex. 126.)\n\nHeading into sentencing, Mara continues to maintain that he never had \u201cany\n\ninappropriate contact with the alleged victims.\u201d PSR at p. 21.\n\nII. CALCULATION OF THE GUIDELINES RECOMMENDATION\n\nWhile the defendant continues to contest his guilt, he agrees that, in light of the jury\u2019s\n\nverdict, the probation office has correctly calculated his recommended sentence under\n\nSentencing Guidelines. Mara\u2019s final offense level is 49, which defaults to 43 \u2013 the highest score\n\non the sentencing table. PSR at \u00b6\u00b6 69-70. The Guidelines recommendation is a life sentence. PSR\n\nat \u00b6 102.\n\n4\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 5 of 16\n\nIII. APPLICATION OF THE SENTENCING FACTORS IN U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a)\n\nUnder 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a), when imposing a sentence, a court should consider: (1) the\n\nnature and circumstances of the offense; (2) the history and characteristics of a defendant; (3) the\n\nneed for the sentence imposed to promote the goals of sentencing, including promoting respect\n\nfor the law, providing just punishment for the offense, affording adequate deterrence to criminal\n\nconduct, and protecting the public from further crimes of the defendant; (4) the sentencing\n\nguideline range; (5) the kinds of sentences available and range of supervised release; (6) the need\n\nto avoid unwarranted sentence disparities among defendants with similar records who have been\n\nfound guilty of similar conduct; and (7) the need to provide restitution to any victims of the\n\noffense.\n\n\u201cIn all sentencing proceedings . . . district courts are required to use the Guidelines\u2019\n\nadvisory range as \u2018the starting point and the initial benchmark.\u2019\u201d United States v. Abed, 3 F.4th\n\n104, 118 (4th Cir. 2021) (quoting Gall v. United States, 552 U.S. 38, 49 (2007)). Before imposing\n\na sentence outside the Guidelines\u2019 range, the Court \u201cmust consider the extent of the deviation and\n\nensure that the justification is sufficiently compelling to support the degree of the variance.\u201d\n\nUnited States v. Zuk, 874 F.3d 398, 409 (4th Cir. 2017).\n\nHere, Mara\u2019s Guidelines recommendation is life, and as explained below, there are no\n\nfactors that would justify a downward variance.\n\nA. The Nature and Circumstances of the Offenses\n\nMara has been convicted of aggravated sexual abuse of a child \u2013 easily one of the most\n\nheinous offenses under federal law. He was convicted of four counts of that offense, reflecting\n\nfour separate assaults against two victims. He was also convicted of attempting to entice a minor\n\n5\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 6 of 16\n\nto engage in unlawful sex and of attempting to obstruct justice by covering up his sexual abuse\n\nof the minor victims. The seriousness of these offenses is difficult to overstate.\n\nMara\u2019s crimes were cold and calculated. He was not momentarily overcome with the\n\ndesire to commit sexual assault. Rather, he groomed his victims, exploiting their poverty and\n\nvulnerability, and using his position of relative authority and wealth to make the victims\n\ndependent on him. His sexual assaults were repeated, violent, and brazen \u2013 in one instance,\n\nforcing himself on MV2 while a young boy was watching TV right in front of them.\n\nMinor Victim 1 reports in her impact statement that the sexual assaults caused her\n\nimmediate physical pain and bleeding as well as emotional and psychological trauma that\n\ncontinues to this day. Ex. 1 (MV1 Impact Statement) at 1. Being raped and having her virginity\n\ntaken by Mara left her feeling \u201cdefiled\u201d and with a deep sense of having \u201clost something\n\nprecious.\u201d Id. at 2. She is \u201cconstantly overwhelmed by a sense of guilt,\u201d including over her belief\n\nthat she failed to protect MV2. Id. at 1. She notes that her relationship with MV2 is now marred\n\nby embarrassment and mistrust. Id. Often, MV1\u2019s memories of the sexual assaults overwhelm\n\nher thoughts, causing her to break down crying, even while in school. Id. She feels that her\n\nfamily can no longer rely on strangers for help, given the trust they placed in Mara that was later\n\n\u201cshattered.\u201d Id. She fears that if Mara is released from prison, he will find her and seek\n\nretaliation. Id. at 2.\n\nMinor Victim 2 reports in her own impact statement that when Mara raped her, it caused\n\nher \u201cextreme pain\u201d that made it difficult for her to walk home afterward. Ex. 2 (MV2 impact\n\nstatement) at 2. She then suffered headaches and dizziness, which she continues to experience.\n\nId. Her psychological trauma is also ongoing. She feels ashamed of herself and believes she is\n\n\u201cno longer . . . like other girls [her] age.\u201d Id. at 1. She even believes that God will not forgive her\n\n6\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 7 of 16\n\nfor being raped by Mara. Id. She worries about her parents or future husband learning the truth\n\nabout what Mara did, and she cannot concentrate in class, which has hurt her academic\n\nperformance. Id. at 1. About facing Mara in the future, she writes, \u201cI am always afraid that he\n\nmay return to harm us.\u201d Id. at 2.\n\nThe \u201cnature of and circumstances of the offense\u201d go hand-in-hand with the statutory\n\nsentencing goals of ensuring the sentence \u201creflect[s] the seriousness of the offense\u201d and\n\n\u201cprovide[s] just punishment for the offense.\u201d 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a)(2)(A). Severe sentences are\n\nrequired to provide just punishment for extremely serious offenses. The egregious nature of\n\nMara\u2019s crimes and the harm he has caused the victims weigh heavily in favor of a Guidelines-\n\ncompliant life sentence.\n\nB. The History and Characteristics of the Defendant\n\nMara\u2019s history and characteristics also support the government\u2019s requested sentence.\n\nThey show that Mara\u2019s actions were not aberrational. While Mara may point to his lack of\n\ncriminal history, he cannot credibly claim that these assaults do not represent who he is as a\n\nperson. He tormented MV1 and MV2 for a year, and he did so to satisfy his sexual interest in\n\nminor girls, which was evident from not only his actions but also his internet history. Mara\n\nsought out pornographic images of girls aged 13 to 15 \u2013 the same ages as MV1 and MV2.\n\nMara\u2019s personal background does not mitigate his crimes, and if anything, serves as an\n\naggravating factor further supporting a Guidelines sentence. Mara testified at trial that he opened\n\na karate school in Mali that served hundreds of children, and that in Burkina Faso, he taught self-\n\ndefense at a young women\u2019s organization. He told the probation office that while living in\n\nBaltimore, he served as a mentor to youth whose second language was English. PSR at \u00b6 92. The\n\nCourt should view these supposed good works through the lens of Mara\u2019s proven sexual interest\n\n7\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 8 of 16\n\nin minors. Mara\u2019s propensity to find himself among children is an alarming fact that highlights\n\nhis future dangerousness.\n\nMara testified at trial and told the probation office that he suffered extreme hardship\n\nwhen growing up in Guinea, enduring poverty, neglect, beatings, gang rape, and slavery. PSR at\n\n\u00b6\u00b6 80-82. The government has little doubt that Mara grew up in extreme poverty, but the rest of\n\nhis claims are uncorroborated and should be viewed with some skepticism, given Mara\u2019s\n\npropensity to embellish and lie. For example, Mara told the probation office that while in jail\n\nawaiting trial, he was \u201calmost beaten to death.\u201d PSR at \u00b6 87. Records from the jail show that\n\nafter the assault, Mara\u2019s CAT scans and x-rays were negative, and he was discharged with\n\nprescriptions for over-the-counter pain medication and eyedrops. Id. And of course, at trial, Mara\n\nperjured himself repeatedly.\n\nEven assuming Mara did endure a harrowing upbringing that included desperate poverty\n\nand sexual victimization, those points do not mitigate the abuse he inflicted upon MV1 and\n\nMV2. Rather, they show that he understood the girls\u2019 vulnerability and the trauma they would\n\nsuffer from sexual assault, and he raped them nonetheless.\n\nC. Deterrence, Respect for the Law, and Protection of the Public\n\nA lifelong sentence is critical to protecting the public from Mara, who has shown no\n\nwillingness to reckon with his sexual attraction to children or the horrific sexual assaults he\n\nperpetrated against the victims in this case. To be sure, Mara has the right to maintain his\n\ninnocence and appeal his conviction. But in sentencing Mara, the Court may also take him for\n\nwhat he is: a convicted child rapist with a proven sexual attraction to minors, who has not only\n\nfailed to accept responsibility for his actions, but has worked relentlessly to conceal his crimes\n\nby attempting to suborn perjury from a witness, concealing physical evidence, and lying to the\n\n8\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 9 of 16\n\njury. What reassurance can he provide the Court that he will change his ways? The defense may\n\nargue that even with the mandatory minimum sentence, Mara will be a much older man upon\n\nrelease, but this should be cold comfort to the Court. First arrested and detained at age 39, if\n\nMara received a mandatory minimum sentence of 30 years, he would likely serve 85% of that\n\nsentence 3 and be released at about age 64.\n\nScholarly research shows that sexual predators like Mara can continue committing sex\n\noffenses when they are older:\n\nGenerally, older sex offenders engage in more passive sexual activity as compared\nto their younger counterparts. For example, fondling by elderly offenders is more\nprevalent than intercourse. Research has shown that elder offenders are more likely\nto commit \u2018nonviolent\u2019 sexual offenses such as pedophilia or exhibitionism as\nopposed to \u2018violent\u2019 offenses such as forcible rape. Specific examples of these\n\u2018nonviolent\u2019 offenses include improperly touching an acquaintance, statutory rape,\nexposing of the genitals (usually to a minor), and other acts of exhibitionism.\n\nMatt Hart, The Geriatric Sex Offender: Senile or Pedophile?, 32 Law & Psychol. Rev. 153 (2008).\n\nIndeed, a simple Google search reveals numerous news reports of older individuals committing\n\nsex crimes against children.\n\n\u2022 \u201c81-year-old man arrested for allegedly molesting child in his family\u201d -\nhttps://www.kiro7.com/news/local/police-81-year-old-man-arrested-allegedly-molesting-\nchild-his-family/3VJTNTY6KBHPJA33UUEFKPQKAY/, July 21, 2021.\n\u2022 \u201c79-year-old . . . man admits to molesting children, arrested on 25 counts of lewd acts\u201d -\nhttps://www.news9.com/story/5e347136527dcf49dad6f4e3/79yearold-mwc-man-admits-\nto-molesting-children-arrested-on-25-counts-of-lewd-acts, September 10, 2019.\n\u2022 \u201c79-year-old York man who put his child victims through \u2018terrible sexual abuse\u2019\u201d -\nhttps://yorkmix.com/jailed-79-year-old-york-man-who-put-his-child-victims-through-\nterrible-sexual-abuse/, March 12, 2021.\n\u2022 78-year-old \u201cCherokee County, N.C. [man] charged with sexually assaulting children\u201d -\nhttps://wlos.com/news/local/cherokee-county-nc-men-charged-with-sexually-assaulting-\nchildren, March 15, 2021.\n\u2022 \u201c78-year-old OKC man accused of molesting 4-year-old\u201d -\nhttps://www.news9.com/story/5e347840527dcf49dad72629/78yearold-okc-man-accused-\nof-molesting-4yearold, May 17, 2019.\n\n3\nSee 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3624(b)(1).\n9\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 10 of 16\n\nMoreover, there are dozens of examples of defendants over the age of 60 who have been\n\nsentenced in federal court for child exploitation offenses. Hans Bartsch, a 72-year-old man traveled\n\nfrom Canada to have sex with a 14-year-old girl in Tennessee. United States v. Hans Bartsch, 3:10-\n\ncr-00300 (M.D. Tenn. March 12, 2012). Rennie Wells, who was in his mid-60\u2019s, molested three\n\nvery young children while visiting family. United States v. Rennie Wells, 3:13-cr-00212 (M.D.\n\nTenn. September 15, 2014). In Alabama, Gordon Elton Hayes was 60 years old when he produced\n\nchild pornography of three minor victims. United States v. Hayes, 4:07-cr-62 (N.D. Ala.)\n\n(sentenced to 3,720 months). Benton Stong was 74 years old at the time he produced child\n\npornography involving 10- to 12-year-old victims in Iowa. United States v. Stong, 6:13-cr-214\n\n(N.D. Iowa.) (sentenced to 1,320 months).\n\nThere are also plenty of examples of older defendants who re-offended even after serving\n\nprison sentences. Jerry Lee Hendricks was 66 years old when he was convicted of producing child\n\npornography of one victim in Illinois after previously being convicted in South Carolina of \u201cLewd\n\nacts on a child under 16\u201d when he was 56 years old. See United States v. Hendricks, 12-CR-20025\n\n(C.D. Ill. June 26, 2014). Other examples abound. See United States v. McGee, 10-CR-162, Doc.\n\nNo. 71 (S.D. Ala. Mar. 3, 2011) (62-year-old defendant sentenced to life for production of child\n\npornography following prior sex conviction); United States v. Pavulak, 09-CR-43, Doc. Nos. 1,\n\n120, 121 (D. Del. Oct. 14, 2011) (67-year-old defendant sentenced to life for production of child\n\npornography following prior sex conviction).\n\nSimply put, Mara has given this Court no reason to believe he is capable of rehabilitation.\n\nHe is too dangerous to be released, even at an advanced age.\n\nA significant sentence also serves important general deterrence goals. Congress has long\n\nrecognized the problem of \u201cAmerican citizens . . . using the channels of foreign commerce to travel\n\n10\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 11 of 16\n\nto countries where \u2018dire poverty and . . . lax enforcement\u2019 . . . allow them to \u2018escape prosecution\u2019\n\nfor their crimes of child sexual abuse.\u201d United States v. Pendleton, 658 F.3d 299, 310 (3d Cir. 2011)\n\n(quoting 148 Cong. Rec. 3884). In Burkina Faso, violence against women is prevalent, and rape\n\nhas been \u201cwidely underreported in part due to societal taboos and the drawn-out judicial process\n\nowing to the overburdened justice system.\u201d Burkina Faso 2022 Human Rights Report, U.S.\n\nDepartment of State, at 26. 4 Children from poor families have been particularly vulnerable to sex\n\ntrafficking, and while Burkinabe law criminalizes the sexual exploitation of children, the\n\ngovernment regularly reports no convictions under those laws. Id. at 33.\n\nThis case is far more serious than that of the average American child-sex tourist. Fode Mara\n\nwas an employee of the United States Embassy who carried a diplomatic passport. He lived in a\n\nlarge home leased by the U.S. government, surrounded by high walls, with a uniformed guard on\n\nduty at all hours. He had wealth, status, and power, none of which the victims possessed.\n\nThe victims did not think they would be believed if they accused an American Embassy\n\nemployee of child rape. They did not think anyone could stop their abuse or bring them justice.\n\nAnd they believed (and still believe) that speaking up about their abuse would lead to retaliation\n\nfrom Mara. A significant sentence in this case will send an important message to Americans abroad,\n\nparticularly those who enjoy the power and prestige of representing the U.S. government, that the\n\nsexual exploitation of children overseas will not be tolerated any more than the sexual abuse of\n\nchildren in the U.S. It will also send a powerful message to young victims abroad: that the U.S.\n\ngovernment will pursue justice on their behalf and protect them from American sexual predators.\n\n4\nAvailable at https://www.state.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/415610_BURKINA-FASO-\n2022-HUMAN-RIGHTS-REPORT.pdf, last visited February 12, 2026.\n11\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 12 of 16\n\nD. Avoiding Unwarranted Sentencing Disparities\n\nThe government\u2019s recommended sentence will ensure the avoidance of \u201cunwarranted\n\nsentencing disparities among defendants with similar records who have been found guilty of\n\nsimilar conduct,\u201d per 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 3553(a)(6). Section 3553(a)(6) \u201caims to promote national\n\nuniformity in sentencing,\u201d United States v. Patterson, 755 F. App\u2019x 238, 241 (4th Cir. 2018),\n\nwhile the Sentencing Guidelines themselves were enacted \u201cto ensure some measure of\n\nuniformity in federal sentencing.\u201d United States v. Stewart, 49 F.3d 121, 123 n. 3 (4th Cir. 1995).\n\nFor that reason, as some courts have recognized, \u201cSection 3553(a)(6) is . . . an improper vehicle\n\nfor challenging a within-guidelines sentence.\u201d United States v. Medlin, 65 F.4th 326, 334 (6th\n\nCir. 2023). \u201c[A] within-Guidelines range sentence is presumptively reasonable,\u201d United States v.\n\nWhite, 850 F.3d 667, 674 (4th Cir. 2017), in part because by following the Guidelines, the\n\nsentencing court has necessarily taken appropriate steps to avoid unwarranted sentencing\n\ndisparities. Life sentences are not uncommon for \u00a7 2241(c) convictions and have even been\n\nimposed on defendants who (unlike Mara) accepted responsibility by pleading guilty, and, like\n\nMara, had a criminal history category of I. See, e.g., United States v. Faulkner, No. 3:17-CR-\n\n00045, 2019 WL 1579593, at *1 (E.D. Va. Apr. 12, 2019).\n\nA life sentence is particularly appropriate for Mara, whose \u201ccriminal culpability [i]s\n\nliterally off the charts, requiring his Guidelines offense level of [49] to be reduced to the level 43\n\nmaximum.\u201d United States v. Devine, 40 F.4th 139, 154 (4th Cir. 2022). See PSR \u00b6\u00b6 69-70.\n\nIV. RESTITUTION\n\nUnder 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2248(a), \u201cNotwithstanding section 3663 or 3663A, and in addition to\n\nany other civil or criminal penalty authorized by law, the court shall order restitution for any\n\noffense under this chapter.\u201d (emphasis added). \u201cThis chapter\u201d refers to Chapter 109A, which\n\n12\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 13 of 16\n\nincludes the lead offenses of conviction here \u2013 aggravated sexual abuse of a child under 18\n\nU.S.C. \u00a7 2241(c). The restitution order \u201cshall direct the defendant to pay the victim (through the\n\nappropriate court mechanism) the full amount of the victim\u2019s losses as determined by the court.\u201d\n\n18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2248(b)(1). The \u201c\u2018full amount of the victim\u2019s losses\u2019 includes any costs incurred by\n\nthe victim for medical services relating to physical, psychiatric, or psychological care.\u201d Id. at \u00a7\n\n2248(b)(3)(A). \u201cThis amount also includes restitution for future medical expenses.\u201d United\n\nStates v. Johnston, 707 F. Supp. 2d 616, 618 (E.D.N.C. 2010) (collecting cases). The statute\n\nreiterates that \u201c[t]he issuance of a restitution order under this section is mandatory,\u201d and that the\n\nCourt \u201cmay not decline to issue an order under this section because of the economic\n\ncircumstances of the defendant.\u201d Id. at \u00a7 2248(b)(4)(A) and (B)(i).\n\nMinor Victims 1 and 2 will likely endure lifelong emotional and psychological trauma\n\nbecause of Mara\u2019s abuse. As detailed in Exhibit 3, an affidavit from Burkinabe social worker\n\nAdelaide Sawadogo, the victims \u201care likely to require two mental health counseling sessions per\n\nmonth\u201d until at least the age of 26. Ex. 3 at 2. This is a conservative estimate, given what we\n\nknow about victims of child sexual abuse. For example, one study found that \u201cyoung adult\n\nsubjects exposed to repeated bouts of CSA [child sexual abuse] had high lifetime prevalence\n\nrates for major depression and post-traumatic stress disorder[.]\u201d Teicher, et al., Length of Time\n\nbetween Onset of Childhood Sexual Abuse and Emergence of Depression in a Young Adult\n\nSample, 70 JOURNAL OF CLINICAL PYSCHIATRY 684\u2013691 (2009). 5 See also Fisher, et al., The\n\nimpacts of child sexual abuse: A rapid evidence assessment, INDEPENDENT INQUIRY INTO CHILD\n\nSEXUAL ABUSE 11 (2017) (\u201c[L]ong-term longitudinal research suggests that \u2013 in many cases \u2013\n\nthese adverse outcomes are not just experienced over the short and medium term following\n\n5\nAvailable at https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4266432/.\n13\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 14 of 16\n\nabuse, but instead can endure over a victim and survivor\u2019s lifetime.\u201d). 6 The proposed care plan\n\nwould provide counseling services to the victims only until about the time that their now-\n\nadolescent brains become fully developed. See Arain, M., et al., Maturation of the Adolescent\n\nBrain. NEUROPSYCHIATRIC DISEASE AND TREATMENT, 9, 449\u2013461. 7\n\nMs. Sawadogo estimates that the cost of the proposed treatment plan will be 4,800,000\n\nFCFA for MV1 and 5,400,000 FCFA for MV2. Ex. 3 at 3. Under the exchange rate at the time\n\nthe affidavit was signed, this amounted to a total of $18,214.28. Id. Using the U.S. Treasury\n\nDepartment\u2019s Currency Exchange Rates Converter, 8 and applying the most recent published date\n\navailable (December 31, 2025), the conversions are as follows:\n\nMV1: 4,800,000 FCFA = $8,668.17\nMV2: 5,400,000 FCFA = $9,751.69\nTotal restitution: $18,419.86.\n\n\u201cAny dispute as to the proper amount or type of restitution shall be resolved by the court\n\nby the preponderance of the evidence,\u201d with the burden placed on the government. 18 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7\n\n2248(b)(2), 3664(e). The government submits that Ms. Sawadogo\u2019s affidavit and the academic\n\nliterature cited herein easily establish by a preponderance of the evidence that these restitution\n\namounts are accurate. The Court should, therefore, order the defendant to pay $18,419.86 in\n\nrestitution.\n\nV. CONCLUSION\n\nThe defendant\u2019s crimes are egregious and merit an equally serious punishment of life\n\nimprisonment. A life sentence would be compliant with the advisory Sentencing Guidelines and\n\n6\nAvailable at https://www.iicsa.org.uk/key-documents/1534/view/iicsa-impacts-child-sexual-\nabuse-rapid-evidence-assessment-full-report-english.pdf.\n7\nAvailable at https://doi.org/10.2147/NDT.S39776.\n8\nAvailable at https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/currency-exchange-rates-converter/.\n14\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 15 of 16\n\nwould be given a presumption of reasonableness on appeal. The government\u2019s requested\n\nsentence is also the only one that fully protects the public from future offending by Mara, which\n\nshould be of grave concern to the Court, given the nature of Mara\u2019s offenses, his demonstrated\n\nsexual interest in minors, his efforts to conceal his crimes, and the unlikelihood that Mara will\n\never accept responsibility for his actions and attempt to reform himself. To provide justice for the\n\nvictims in this case and to protect the public, the Court should impose the maximum sentence.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nKelly O. Hayes\nUnited States Attorney\n\nBy:\nAdam Braskich\nDepartment of Justice\nTrial Attorney\n\nBrooke Oki\nRanganath Manthripragada\nAssistant United States Attorneys\n\n15\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153 Filed 02/12/26 Page 16 of 16\n\nCERTIFICATE OF SERVICE\n\nI hereby certify that I caused the foregoing to be electronically filed with the Clerk of the\n\nCourt using the CM/ECF system, which will provide notice of such filing to all users.\n\n/s/ Adam Braskich\nAdam Braskich\nTrial Attorney\n\n16\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 1 of 9\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 2 of 9\n\nHave you experienced any of the following reactions to the crime?\nPlease realize these are normal reactions to a traumatic event.\n\nAnger __X_ Anxiety __X_ Fear _X_ Grief _X_ Guilt _X_ Numbness _X_ Depression X__\nSleep Loss _X_ Nightmares _X_ Chronic Fatigue __ Forgetfulness _X_ Trouble Concentrating _X_\n\nOther Reactions: Irritability, frequent urination day and night\n\nHave you or your family gone to therapy or counseling as a result of the crime? Please explain.\n\nAs a victim, I personally attended several psychological support sessions (at least 15 session). During\nthese sessions, I was able to meet with a psychologist who helped me express myself and share my\nexperiences without judgment. The advice and moral support I received helped restore my\nconfidence and gave me the strength and determination to continue fighting. I greatly appreciate this\npsychological support.\n\nAs for my family, they did not attend psychiatric sessions. However, they received support through\nphone calls from the psychologist, during which practical advice was provided, as well as visits and\nmaterial support.\n\nWere you physically injured as a result of the crime? If so, please describe your injuries and\nany medical treatment received.\n\nPhysically, I was injured, as I suffered tears and bleeding during the rape. I experienced severe\nheadaches, nausea, and pain in my lower abdomen.\n\nNo, I did not receive medical care because no one was aware of my situation.\n\nIn addition to this, I felt a deep sense of being defiled, of having lost something precious.\n\nDo you feel the defendant continues to be a threat to you, your family or the community?\n\nCertainly, the accused still represents a threat to me, as I fear that once released, he may find me and\nseek retaliation. As for my family, they share the same fears, as they did not respect anonymity\ndespite the recommendations made regarding the accused.\n\nFor the community, we fear that other minors like me may suffer the same fate.\n\nWhat are your thoughts regarding the sentence the Court should impose on the defendant?\n\nAs for me, I fully rely on the Court regarding the sentence.\n\n2\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 3 of 9\n\nRestitution:\n\nHave you or anyone on your behalf initiated civil action against any party as a result of this offense?\n\nYES______ NO_______\n\nIf YES, please state the case name, docket number and court of jurisdiction:\n\nPlease list any and all damages and or expenses incurred as a result of this crime.\nItemize each expense individually and total the amount. Include medical expenses, including\ncounseling, lost, or damaged property, lost wages, etc. Include receipts or other documentation\nwhere possible. You may be asked to testify as to the losses listed on your impact statement, so\nyour statements must be accurate and truthful. This information is important in order to\nrequest proper restitution for you at sentencing. It does not guarantee that restitution will be\nordered or that it will be paid immediately. But, without this information, restitution cannot\noccur.\n\nThe psychological counseling was covered by the KEOGO Association.\n\nIs there anything else you would like the Judge to know? If you need additional space for any\nquestion, please attach an additional sheet of paper or write on the back of this page.\n\nx I respectfully request that appropriate compensation be granted at the judge\u2019s discretion.\nx There is a need to continue the psychological support.\n\nSignature: ___________________________________ Date: _______________________\n\n3\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 4 of 9\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 5 of 9\n\n5\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 6 of 9\n\n6\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 7 of 9\n\n7\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 8 of 9\n\n8\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-1 Filed 02/12/26 Page 9 of 9\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 1 of 8\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 2 of 8\n\nwhom I can share my problems and receive comfort. My family also received visits and guidance at\nhome and at school.\n\nWere you physically injured as a result of the crime? If so, please describe your injuries and\nany medical treatment received.\n\nOn the day he forced himself on me, I experienced severe pain and had difficulty walking home.\nAfter that, I suffered from headaches and dizziness, which continue to this day. At the time of the\nincident, I did not receive medical care, but later I went for consultations. Even this month of\nDecember, I was accompanied by Keogo for a medical consultation and to undergo medical\nexaminations.\n\nDo you feel the defendant continues to be a threat to you, your family or the community?\n\nI am always afraid that he may return to harm us.\n\nWhat are your thoughts regarding the sentence the Court should impose on the defendant?\n\nI prefer that the court decide.\n\nRestitution:\n\nHave you or anyone on your behalf initiated civil action against any party as a result of this offense?\n\nYES______ NO_______\n\nIf YES, please state the case name, docket number and court of jurisdiction:\n\nPlease list any and all damages and or expenses incurred as a result of this crime.\nItemize each expense individually and total the amount. Include medical expenses, including\ncounseling, lost, or damaged property, lost wages, etc. Include receipts or other documentation\nwhere possible. You may be asked to testify as to the losses listed on your impact statement, so\nyour statements must be accurate and truthful. This information is important in order to\nrequest proper restitution for you at sentencing. It does not guarantee that restitution will be\nordered or that it will be paid immediately. But, without this information, restitution cannot\noccur.\n\nIs there anything else you would like the Judge to know? If you need additional space for any\nquestion, please attach an additional sheet of paper or write on the back of this page.\n\n--\n\nSignature: ___________________________________ Date: _______________________\n\n2\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 3 of 8\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 4 of 8\n\n4\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 5 of 8\n\n5\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 6 of 8\n\n6\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 7 of 8\n\n7\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-2 Filed 02/12/26 Page 8 of 8\nKE.OOGO\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-3 Filed 02/12/260l BPPage\n6213 OUAGADOl'GOl'\n1 of 3 01\nBURKINA FASO\nTEL: (00-226) 25 38 03 24 /Ex Sectcur 15\nE-mail: keooqo@yahoo.fr\nWEB / www.keoogo.bf\n\nUnited States v. Fode Mara.\n\nAFFIDAVIT OF ADELAIDE SAWADOGO RE: RESTITUTION\nI, the undersigned, Mrs. Adelaide Safiatou Sawadogo, a social worker employed as a\nspecialist educator within the Keoogo Association, hereby declare under penalty of\nperjury pursuant to the laws of the United States that the following statements are\ntruthful and accurate.\nBackground\n\nFor over 25 years, I have worked in the field of victim support and advocacy for child\nvictims of sex trafficking and other forms of sexual exploitation and abuse. I hold\ndegrees in sociology and social work and certifications in trauma informed interview\ntechniques; mental health crisis intervention; suicide prevention among youth; and\nthe support and rehabilitation of sex trafficking victims. I have received certifications\nfrom the most prominent international organizations working to end gender-based\nviolence, including PLAN international, the International Organization for\nMigration, UNICEF, TDHL, SAVE THE CHILDREN, OIM, UNFPA, AIDOS, and\nECPAT INTERNATIONAL. Among my peers in Burkina Faso, I hold the highest\nqualifications to support children in crisis and provide mental health counseling to\nvictims of sexual trauma. Since 2008, I have worked for the Keoogo Association, the\nmission of which is to rescue, protect, repatriate and rehabilitate child victims of sex\ntrafficking. In my professional career, I have provided counseling services to\nthousands of child victims of sexual abuse and exploitation. In 2019, the U.S. State\nDepartment recognized my work with child victims of sexual exploitation by\nawarding me with the prestigious International Trafficking in Persons Report Hero\nAward, presented to me by the Secretary of State in Washington, D.C.\nIn April 2024, the United States Department of Justice requested that I meet with\ntwo Burkinabe girls who were suspected victims of sexual abuse. The following\nmonth, with the assistance of U.S. Embassy personnel in Ouagadougou, Burkina\nFaso, I met with the girls - Minor Victim 1 (MVl) and Minor Victim 2 (MV2).\n\nAfter holding several meetings with the girls, they began to disclose to me that the\ndefendant, Fode Mara, had sexually assaulted them. In late May 2024, I accompanied\nthe girls to the U.S. Embassy and assisted as they were interviewed by a federal agent\nand a prosecutor. With great difficulty, the girls disclosed their abuse to the agent\nand prosecutor. After Fode Mara was arrested in June 2024, I kept in regular contact\nwith MVl and MV2 and provided them mental health counseling.\n\n1IIIIIIIIL___\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-3 Filed 02/12/26 Page 2 of 3\n\nIn October 2025, I accompanied lv.[\\11 and MV2 to the United States for trial\nWith them during numerous trial preparation sessions with prosecutor\ufffd:\n.\nwhich the minors recounted their abuse. The Court appointed me to se1,\u00b7ve\"'\"\nattendant to the minors, and I therefore sat beside them in court while\nbefore the jury about their sexual abuse. I provided counseling and\u00b7\u00b7\nminors immediately after their testimony, and I have remained in contact\nsince they and I returned to Burkina Faso in late October 2025.\n\nRestitution\n\nHaving provided mental health counseling to thousands . of victims \u00b7of\n. Gli:ild\n.\nabuse in Burkina Faso, and having ,vorked close'ly with MVI and\npositioned estimate what level of continuing menhi'l,health treatnient\nwill requll\u00b7e to cope with their abuse. I am also famili;;i.r with the\nin Burkina Faso and can reasonably.eStimate.);l,ie c'qst.that the minor\ntheir family would incur in arrangi1?-g the needed-}\ufffd13._nt'al-hea.lth couns,eling:;';'\n\nMy estimates are as follows:\n\nMVI and 1VN2 continue to ..experien4\ufffd::::emotional and \u00b7psychological\nrequire ongoing supportit'\ufffd\u00b7\"\u00b7;\ufffdb\\iil\ufffdHheii{\\$\u00a2_1f-esteem, achieve\nand overcome fear of S0cial judgn:{\ufffdhi:/PsY{Ji0iogical care is essential, but\naccompanied by assistance addressing\u00b7\u00b7 \ufffd\u00a3h,\ufffdr ne-\ufffddii:\ufffd'tated to their\nas food, health care, hygf\ufffdp,e, school exp\ufffd;\ufffd-\ufffd\ufffds, and dignity-preserving\n\n.\nBased o:ri \"fhe above:\n\nMVl (born in 2008).,y,ill turn}$ in March 2026. Support should continue\nreac_hes the age of 26:\"ieP.:t\ufffd\ufffd$\ufffd\ufffdting an additional 8 years of assistance\n\nMV2_ (b,o_r,n in 2009) will.{::n 17 on April 12, 2026. Support should\nreac.h_e/t'lie::'.a_ge\u00b7 o()\ufffd..ge 26, representing an additional 9 years of\n2035),\n\nBoth victii\u00b5s are likely to require two mental health counseling sessions rrer\u00b7rroc\nthroughout the support period. At a 1\u00b7ate of 50,000 FCFA per month (tllJU, IU.LJlJ.,\nper year) for each victim, and with an emergency/contingency fund of\nper quarter (100,000 FCFA per year) per victim. Thus, the annual cost\nonly per victim is 600,000 FCFA.\n\n2\nCase 8:24-cr-00187-LKG Document 153-3 Filed 02/12/26 Page 3 of 3\n\nGiven the extended support period-8 years for MVl and 9 years for MV2-the total\nestimated cost for both victims is as follows:\n\nMVl: 8 years x 600,000 FCFA = 4,800,000 FCFA\nMV2: 9 years x 600,000 FCFA = 5,400,000 FCFA\n\nCombined total: 10,200,000 FCFA\nAt the current exchange rate, this equates to approxim 'Z.18,214.28 USD.\n--.,_.\n\n.--.Cic,tt . :\nPo'-:\u2022,\n,\\,rt,\\. ,;:.\n\ufffd\\\n\nMme SAWADOGO S. Adelaide\n\nI solemnly affirm, under penalty purs United States,\n.__.\nthat I have well and truly interpreted the origi this affidavit to\nEnglish. ........_==--\n\nDaouda Z. Ouedraogo\nForeign Service National Investigator\nU.S. Embassy, Ouagadougou, Burkina Faso\n\n3"
